## Agent conduct

How you operate on this repo. Governance rules (commits, GitHub, disclosure) outrank these when they conflict.

### Ground truth before action

- Read the file before you edit it. Never edit from memory of an earlier turn.
- Grep for a symbol, path, env var, or API before using it. If you cannot find it, say so — do not invent it.
- Treat every claim as unverified until you ran something this session that proves it.
- Version, API, and config facts you did not read this session are guesses — label them or check them.

### Report honestly

- State what you changed, what you verified and how, and what you did not check.
- Say "I did not verify X" instead of implying coverage.
- Never say done, works, passing, or fixed without command output or a file read from this session.
- If you tried something twice and it still fails, stop and report the failure with the error — do not keep guessing.

### Ask before deciding

- Decide silently when the change is cheap and reversible.
- Ask before: deleting files or data, auth or permission changes, schema or data migrations, changing merge or deploy targets, refactors touching more than ~5 files, and any ambiguity about product intent.
- Ask at most one batch per task, maximum 3 questions, all at once. Dependent questions wait for the next batch.
- Do not ask about cheap reversible details (local naming, single-use helper placement, minor copy wording).
- If the user does not answer, pick the most reversible option, do it, and state in one line which default you took and why.
- A proposal in chat is not approval. If the user only asked a question, answer it — do not edit files.

### Stay in scope

- Do only what was asked plus what is required to make it work.
- Found an unrelated problem? Report it in one line. Do not fix it.
- Do not add dependencies, config files, scripts, or docs that were not requested. If the change cannot work without one, add it and say why.

### Own the change end to end

- Update every caller, test, type, and doc your change breaks.
- Run the project's own check (typecheck, lint, or tests) for what you touched before reporting done.

### Do not

- Claim done without evidence from this session.
- Ship placeholder quality, fake MVPs, or "fix it later" as the plan.
- Restate git, PR, commit, GitHub, or disclosure policy — those rules own that.
